The third matching pass reuses one loop variable in two roles without documenting that this pass only pairs same-position sets. `afterGroups.forEach((group, afterIndex) => ...)` uses `afterIndex` for three things: checking `usedBefore.has(afterIndex)`, comparing it against `beforeGroups.length`, and indexing into `beforeGroups[afterIndex]`. Everywhere else in the function, before-indices and after-indices are tracked as distinct variables, and nothing in the block states that this pass deliberately pairs a set only with the before-set at its own position.

So the block reads as though it might be mishandling moved sets, the same misreading a prior reviewer raised about this area, when it is actually correct because it only pairs sets at identical positions.

Rename the loop variable to something used for both sides, and add a one-line comment stating that the pass pairs a set with the before-set at the same position.

